for this week's classic interview we  will go back to may of 2014 when i  interviewed Univision host Fernando  espuelas and the topic was how can  Republicans gain minority votes the  context then was about the midterm  election and more generally I think that  this interview really applies to what is  going on now with the Republican Party  Trump implosion and disaster aside  there's this broader issue with the  Republican Party that has been building  for a long time which is you can't rely  overly on white voters because white  voters are a smaller and smaller share  of the electorate every election and I  talked with Fernando about is it even  possible for Republicans to make changes  to attract minority voters let's think  about this in the context of this  upcoming election i'm joined today by  fernando s policies the host and  managing editor of the Fernando espuelas  show on Univision America radio network  it's great to have you on I'll actually  listen to your show which isn't always  the case you know with with a lot of our  guests and it's really cool you actually  do your show partly in Spanish partly in  English how do you decide which parts to  do in which language yeah well thanks so  much for inviting me on your program  well our concept is that Latinos are  predominantly now bilingual and  therefore we can go back and forth when  it makes sense to do so and we will not  lose our audience and in fact that's  what's happened so what we do is we  default Spanish and because it's a  spanish-language network but when we  have callers who are not comfortable  speaking Spanish we switched to English  or when I'm interviewing a political  figure which mostly they don't speak  Spanish we just switch into English and  that's basically the format it totally  depends on what we're talking about and  then there are moments when I forget  Spanish moment so I switched into  English and that seems to work okay as  well well it's it's hugely interesting  to me to do a show that is a political  show that is uh is mostly or at least  part in Spanish because the Hispanic  vote is really becoming increasingly  important and more and more of a topic  of discussion going into the 2016  election and beyond and I've talked  about for years how their demographic  at play in the u.s. right now that are  going to make it really difficult for  Republicans to continue the strategy of  this over reliance on the white vote I  mean looking at 2016 Republicans based  on demographics would need it could be  as much as 60 or 62 percent of the white  vote if they don't gain some of the  non-white vote where do you see this  right now and people you talk to what is  the what is the primary issue that is  keeping Hispanic voters away from voting  for republicans with the exception of  course of cuban-americans which are the  most conservative of the different  Hispanic groups in the US well I'm I  think that I agree with your basic math  that it's increasingly hard to see how  Republicans win the white house without  attracting a much bigger percentage of  the minority vote in general including  african-americans including Asian  Americans but certainly the Hispanic  vote is a critical component because it  is although it's underrepresented in the  voting the overall numbers of potential  voters is huge and it's growing by  50,000 kids who turn 18 every single  month so the issue I think it's a policy  issue i think that the republican party  has shrunk to now be primarily an older  obviously there are other people who  vote for them but generally speaking  that's what is and they've appealed to  this electorate primarily through a  strategy of fear in 2009 if you remember  the Tea Party movement which was  essentially created by consultants in  Washington and then funded by some big  money interest a lot of their messaging  was a give our country back give us our  country back and then bizarre depictions  of Obama's either both a Nazi and a  communist note with no apparent irony  involved so I think that the the overall  demographics change of the country is is  a is a factor I think having an  african-american president is a factor I  think that the Tea Party moving the  center of gravity of the Republican  Party to the far right is the factor  and at the end of the day what's  happened I believe is that you know  Republicans are in favor of immigration  reform if you look at national polling  but when you get into the Congressional  party which has been gerrymandered into  these perfectly hermetically sealed  homogenous congressional districts  mostly that electorate main may not only  not want immigration reform may feel  threatened by it and certainly the  politicians have exploited that fear  with a tremendous cascade of lies about  illegals and they're robbing your job  and and you know they're criminals and  so forth and so on so so I think that  the the problem is that this the  Republican Party does not have today it  seems the will to fundamental evolve its  positions you know they've lost five out  of the six last elections in terms of  popular vote you know that's probably a  signal that your program is not a  program that well can attract a national  majority and but they go through this  process where they decide they have to  change and when it comes to having to  change they default on essentially a  messaging problem we have a messaging  problem but it's not a messaging problem  when you have Mitt Romney in 2012  running on this you know essentially  this the harshest policy possible  self-deportation old ladies and children  out you know will make their lives  impossible until they leave I'm an  American citizen it doesn't impact me  there's no one in my family whose  impacted by a reform but I was highly  insulted by it by that rhetoric and and  certainly the premise that you would be  able to attract supporters not just  among Hispanics but among Americans  independence and and center Republicans  to what is uh you know vaguely 1930s  policy of displacement of 12 million  people I mean just make some sense I  think that there may also be another  factor at play here always lurking in  the background of all of these positions  is the effect on fundraising right  because fundraising because of our our  how elections are financed here are our  fundraising is is at the forefront is it  possible that Republicans realize that  they need to  their positions on on immigration maybe  adopting something closer to what Jeb  Bush talked about recently saying you  know it's just not really feasible to  say we're going to deport everybody we  should institute some kind of path to  citizenship but at the same time they  realize that their base which is very  much against anything even resembling  amnesty may stop donating money to them  if they adopt that policy you know I'm  not sure that's because the party from  my perspective anyway it seems to be  divided among the grass roots which are  primarily blue class workers you know  older downscale lower education levels  and then the so-called establishment  which are primarily business interests  and the business interests like for  example the US Chamber of Commerce which  has been essentially an annex of the  Republican Party for decades they're  completely in favor of immigration  reform and why because it's very  beneficial to business and I think if  you pull back what is immigration reform  forget the emotions it is a fundamental  shift in how we have to structure our  country in order to be competitive for  the next 100 years if you just were to  freeze magically the population that is  in the country today no more immigrants  legal illegal doesn't matter you would  in fact have a declining population  about 30 years from now set in another  way we would have a declining national  economy a political structure but our  ability to project power internationally  would go away I mean there are a lot of  really fundamental issues here that  immigration addresses you know and then  you can go up and down the scale you can  go to Silicon Valley and how you know  that engine of tremendous innovation  really requires lots of immigrants that  we want to be able to Hoover up the the  best minds of the in the world and have  them come here without all the hassle or  if they're coming to our universities to  have them stay here and then all the way  on the other side of the labor market  the reality of it is and this was put to  the test in Alabama with the  anti-immigrant law there and Georgia at  the same way that without immigrants who  are essentially willing to  do some very hard work that other people  are not willing to do the agricultural  sector suffers tremendously and then  there's everyone else in the middle  right so so I think the issue ultimately  is about ideology more than money and  and I think that the the it's impossible  seemingly at least that's what Romney  experienced to win the Republican  nomination without taking this extremist  position and let's remember Mitt Romney  of all the people on planet earth who he  what he could have picked to be his  immigration advisor he picked Crisco  Bosh who was the author of the Alabama  anti-immigrant law the Arizona  anti-immigrant law who has been writing  voter ID laws to exclude Hispanics and  who can be traced to a group of white  supremacist organizations no I'm not  exaggerating it if you look at John tent  and the John Canton network there's a  great New York Times piece that's worth  reading where it actually puts out all  this these huge groups of supposedly  independent activists or in fact all  connected behind one strategy which is  to stop all immigration into this  country so I mean that was that was Mitt  Romney's choice and and I think that  whatever else you think about his  politics I don't think he's in fact I  think he's a very smart man and and but  at the end of the day in order to get  even close to winning the nomination he  had to assume some of these positions  which were ultimately completely  unpalatable to well Hispanic certainly  but asian-americans he they'd lost the  asian american vote by you know even a  bigger margin than than the hispanic  vote Fernando espuelas Fernando is  fellas show on Univision American radio  network what's the best way for people  around the country to listen to your  show well if you have a Univision  America station in your city that's one  way but probably the best way is to  listen online you can listen to my show  live on euphoria which is a nap on a  Univision app you can download for  whatever phone platform you can also  listen to iHeartRadio online and also  Univision america.com streams the show  as well it's on for  224 eastern time monday thru friday and  then it's all across the country  excellent fernando you're from enugu I  i'm from argentina i'm very excited for  the world cup which is which is going to  be happening soon how are things looking  for your country this time around well i  think of you know I'm the one Uruguayan  who does not follow soccer so so you  know Argentina then I'm gonna root for  the United States and and Uruguay as  well in Argentine you know at the end of  the day I learned early on that I will  not attach my emotions to a bunch of  guys who may or may not win and I cannot  control so I'm not into the heartbreak  experience of the world cup but I  certainly will watch the finals all  right Fernando really a pleasure having  you on thanks for doing it thank David a  pleasure being with you thanks for  inviting me

